<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!--
  =============================================================================
  The Aquarius Desktop — window manager configuration
  =============================================================================
  (An XML comment may not contain two hyphens in a row, which is why the rules
  in this file are drawn with = signs instead of the dashes used everywhere
  else in the project. It is a quirk of XML, not a change of style.)

  This is the AquariusOS copy of the file in the aquarius-shell repository. It
  is the same file with the two-compositors question settled: AquariusOS runs
  labwc, because labwc 0.20 has HDR10 and colour management today and the
  alternative does not. That is standing decision 2b, 2026-09-02.

  CHANGE ONE, CHANGE BOTH, AND IT IS CHECKED NOW. Nothing copies the shell's
  session/labwc/ folder into this image; these are hand-maintained copies. That
  rule was broken twice on 6 September 2026 — the desktop right-click menu in
  the morning, the Super+Tab bindings that afternoon — so as of the second pass
  the build compares the two files directly. Every element and attribute below
  is identical to the shell's copy; only the prose differs, and only because
  each copy is written for its own reader. The comparison is
  build_files/check-labwc-drift.sh, run on every push by CI.

  =============================================================================
  WHY THIS FILE IS SO SHORT
  =============================================================================
  <default /> below loads labwc's own key bindings: Alt+Tab to switch windows,
  Alt+F4 to close, Super+arrows to snap, the volume and brightness keys. Those
  are good and there is no reason to reinvent them, so this file adds three
  bindings on top and otherwise gets out of the way. The same is true of the
  mouse section at the bottom: its own <default /> keeps labwc's window
  dragging, edge-resize, click-to-focus and alt-drag, and the section adds
  exactly one thing on top, a right-click on the desktop.

  (The mouse section's opening and closing tags are deliberately written on
  lines of their own, with nothing else on them, and this comment deliberately
  does not spell them out in prose. The build reads that section on its own to
  check the <default /> is still inside it, and it finds the section by looking
  for a line that is nothing but the tag. Prose that spelled the tag out would
  be found first and the check would read the wrong lines.)

  =============================================================================
  TWO RULES THIS FILE OBEYS
  =============================================================================
  1. NO COLOURS IN THIS FILE. Colour in the Aquarius Desktop lives in the
     shell's theme/Ice.qml and theme/Midnight.qml, and NOWHERE ELSE — not even
     here, and not even in a themerc. There used to be one hand-written
     exception, a file called themerc-override, holding a second copy of the
     Ice palette. It is gone. In its place is generate-theme next to this file,
     which READS the shell's palette and writes labwc's colours out for it. A
     generated copy cannot drift from the palette, because it is made from it
     every time the desktop starts.

  2. NO PATHS. The shell is started from the autostart file next to this one,
     with a bare `qs`, because /usr/bin/aquarius-session exports QS_CONFIG_PATH
     first. Nothing in this folder names a directory.

  =============================================================================
  THIS FILE IS A TEMPLATE. THREE PARTS OF IT ARE WRITTEN BY A PROGRAM.
  =============================================================================
  labwc keeps one look in two places: colours come from a themerc, but the
  corner radius, the fonts and which side the window buttons sit on are rc.xml
  settings and there is no themerc key for any of them. All three have to change
  while the desktop is running — the first two follow AQ_UI_SCALE like every
  other size, and the third follows `aq keys mac` / `aq keys windows` — and this
  file lives in /usr, which on an image-based operating system is read-only.

  So generate-theme next to this file copies this file, rewrites the three
  regions marked

      AQ-GENERATED-BEGIN <name>   ...   AQ-GENERATED-END <name>

  and writes the finished rc.xml into ~/.config/aquarius/labwc/, which is the
  folder /usr/bin/aquarius-session then starts labwc with. Everything outside
  those three regions — every binding, every comment, this one included — is
  copied through untouched.

  The values written between the markers here are the design at AQ_UI_SCALE=1
  with Mac buttons, so this template is a correct, complete rc.xml on its own if
  it is ever read directly. It is, in fact, read directly whenever generating
  fails: the launcher falls back to this folder rather than refusing to start.

  =============================================================================
  KEY BINDINGS ADDED BY US
  =============================================================================
    W-space     open or close the Aquarius search palette
    W-Return    a terminal
    W-Tab       open the Aquarius app switcher and step forward through it
    W-S-Tab     the same, backwards
    W-grave     cycle the windows of the app you are in, with no panel
    A-Tab       the app switcher again, for the Windows keyboard style
    A-S-Tab     the same, backwards
    W-S-e       leave the desktop and return to the login screen
    C-W-q       lock the screen (Mac mode: Ctrl+Command+Q)
    W-l         lock the screen (Windows mode: Win+L)

  =============================================================================
  THE MOUSE BINDING ADDED BY US
  =============================================================================
    Right-click on the empty desktop     the Aquarius menu, from menu.xml

  menu.xml sits next to this file and is what "root-menu" below contains.

  Being exact about the bug the bench photographed on 6 September 2026, because
  the obvious reading of it is wrong: labwc's OWN defaults already send a
  right-click on the desktop to a menu called root-menu. What was missing was
  menu.xml, so root-menu resolved to labwc's built-in fallback and the desktop
  offered Terminal / Reconfigure / Exit. Adding menu.xml is the fix. The binding
  below is written out anyway, for two reasons: it does not leave our desktop's
  most visible gesture depending on a default that could change in a later labwc,
  and it keeps this file line-for-line comparable with the shell's own copy.

  "W" is labwc's letter for Super (the Windows / Command key). Its other
  modifiers are S=shift, C=control, A=alt.

  =============================================================================
  ONE WINDOW RULE
  =============================================================================
  Every new window opens in the middle of its screen — see <placement> just
  below <core>. DaVinci Resolve is shrunk to fit the screen if it asks for
  more; it is the only application in this file named by name, and the long
  reason why is written above the rule itself, at the bottom of the file.

  Format reference: labwc-config(5) and labwc-actions(5), both installed on
  this machine — type `man 5 labwc-config`.
  =============================================================================
-->

<labwc_config>

  <core>
    <!-- Let the window manager draw title bars rather than each application
         drawing its own. Fewer competing visual languages on screen. -->
    <decoration>server</decoration>
    <gap>8</gap>
  </core>

  <!--
    =========================================================================
    WHERE A NEW WINDOW OPENS: IN THE MIDDLE. Added 2026-09-08.
    =========================================================================
    THIS IS A DECISION ABOUT EVERY WINDOW, not just one application, and it is
    written here rather than buried so that nobody is surprised by it.

    labwc's own default is `cascade`: each new window opens a little down and
    to the right of the last one, walking towards the bottom-right corner of
    the screen. That is the X11 habit. `center` puts every new window in the
    middle of the screen it opens on — which is what a Mac does, and what
    AquariusOS is meant to feel like.

    MEASURED, not assumed, on the bench on 2026-09-08: a 600x400 window on a
    1280x720 screen opened at x=472 (cascade, the default) and at x=340 with
    this setting, which is exactly centred. The four policies labwc 0.20.2
    accepts are `center`, `cursor`, `automatic` and `cascade`; `cursor` puts
    the window under the pointer and `automatic` hunts for free space.

    WHY IT IS GLOBAL AND NOT PER-APPLICATION. It has to be. labwc 0.20.2 has
    NO action that centres a window — the full list of actions in the shipped
    binary was read, and `Center` is not in it (labwc answers "Invalid action:
    Center" if you ask). `<placement><policy>` is the only lever there is, and
    it is a global one. That is not a compromise here: centring every new
    window is the Mac behaviour we want anyway.

    WHAT IT DOES NOT TOUCH. Windows that remember their own position, windows
    an application places itself, and anything already on screen. This is
    where a window OPENS, once.
  -->
  <placement>
    <policy>center</policy>
  </placement>

  <!--
    =========================================================================
    THE HALF OF THE LOOK THAT LIVES IN rc.xml
    =========================================================================
    labwc takes COLOURS from a themerc and everything below from here. That is
    labwc's split, not ours, and it is why one design lives in two files.

    All three settings in this section are written by generate-theme next to
    this file. Read the template note at the top of this file for how that
    works.
  -->
  <theme>

    <!--
      The name labwc looks the WINDOW BUTTONS up under.

      This is not a colour scheme and it does not change the look on its own.
      labwc reads its settings from the folder it was started with, but it looks
      for the little round button PICTURES somewhere else entirely: in
      themes/<this name>/labwc/, searched under XDG_DATA_HOME first, then
      ~/.themes, then XDG_DATA_DIRS (labwc-theme(5)). generate-theme draws the
      pictures into exactly that folder, so this name is the thread that ties
      the two together. Change it here and the buttons vanish.

      There is no themerc file in that folder and there does not need to be —
      every colour and size is in the generated themerc-override instead.
    -->
    <name>Aquarius</name>

    <!--
      How round the top corners of a window are.

      12 to match libadwaita's own fixed 12, so that a GTK application's rounded
      window and the frame labwc draws around it agree instead of showing two
      radii a pixel apart. labwc's own default is 8.

      ⚠️ labwc clamps this to one less than the title bar height, so a very
      small title bar quietly reduces it. At the sizes this design uses that
      never happens (38 against 12).
    -->
    <!-- AQ-GENERATED-BEGIN corner-radius -->
    <cornerRadius>12</cornerRadius>
    <!-- AQ-GENERATED-END corner-radius -->

    <!--
      WHICH SIDE THE WINDOW BUTTONS SIT ON.

      This follows the ONE choice made in the Welcome window and has no setting
      of its own:

        Mac      close, minimise, maximise  on the LEFT
        Windows  minimise, maximise, close  on the RIGHT

      Close is the outermost button either way — furthest from the title —
      because it is the one press you cannot take back.

      The syntax is labwc's: exactly one colon splits left from right, commas
      separate the buttons within a side, and the names are labwc's rather than
      English ones (`iconify` is minimise, `max` is maximise).

      The switch is `aq keys mac` or `aq keys windows`, which writes the
      keyboard style, re-runs generate-theme, sets GNOME's matching
      button-layout so the two desktops agree, and tells labwc to re-read its
      files. No logging out.
    -->
    <!-- AQ-GENERATED-BEGIN titlebar-layout -->
    <titlebar>
      <layout>close,iconify,max:</layout>
    </titlebar>
    <!-- AQ-GENERATED-END titlebar-layout -->

    <!--
      THE FONT labwc DRAWS ITS OWN SURFACES IN.

      Inter is the shell's own UI face — its theme/Theme.qml picks it as
      `fontBody`, and every label in the bar, the dock, the menus and the panels
      is drawn in it. Without these lines labwc would draw its menu in "sans",
      whatever that resolves to on the machine, and the desktop menu would be in
      a different typeface from the shell menu two inches above it.

      IF INTER IS NOT INSTALLED labwc falls back to its default on its own,
      which is the same outcome the shell's Theme.pickFont() arranges for
      itself. This image installs Sora, Inter and JetBrains Mono, so on
      AquariusOS it is always there.

      THE SIZES ARE THE SHELL'S, IN PIXELS — but labwc's <size> is in POINTS
      (src/common/font.c: pango_font_description_set_size, which reads points),
      so generate-theme converts each one with pt() = px * 72/96 before writing
      it here. Getting that wrong is what made the right-click menu a third too
      big on the bench, 2026-09-07. The design pixel sizes, matching
      theme/Theme.qml (which sets font.pixelSize, never pointSize):

        MenuItem   19px = Theme.fsBody     — one row of the Aquarius menu
        MenuHeader 15px = Theme.fsCaption  — a menu title, quieter than its items
        ActiveWindow / InactiveWindow
                   13px — the window title, at weight `medium`. Smaller than the
                         bar's own labels on purpose: a title bar should name a
                         window, not announce it. labwc takes a WORD for the
                         weight rather than a number; `medium` is Pango's 500,
                         which is what the design asks for.

      ⚠️ AQ_UI_SCALE REACHES THESE NOW, and it did not before. It used to be
      that /usr/bin/aquarius-session exported that variable, the shell grew, and
      labwc knew nothing about it — so a scaled desktop had an unscaled desktop
      menu. generate-theme writes these numbers, so they scale with everything
      else.

      ⚠️ THE TITLE FONT AND THE TITLE BAR'S HEIGHT ARE CONNECTED, which is not
      obvious. labwc 0.20 removed the setting that named a title bar height, and
      works it out as max(font height, button height) + 2 x padding. The button
      disc (20) is comfortably taller than this font, so the buttons are what
      decide the height and the arithmetic in generate-theme holds. Make this
      font much bigger and the title bar grows with it.

      Attribute ORDER matters in labwc when a font is written on one line —
      labwc-config(5) is explicit that `place` must come before `name` and
      `size`, or the setting lands on every place at once. Child elements have
      no such trap, so these are written as children.
    -->
    <!-- AQ-GENERATED-BEGIN fonts -->
    <font place="MenuItem">
      <name>Inter</name>
      <size>19</size>
    </font>
    <font place="MenuHeader">
      <name>Inter</name>
      <size>15</size>
    </font>
    <font place="ActiveWindow">
      <name>Inter</name>
      <size>13</size>
      <weight>medium</weight>
    </font>
    <font place="InactiveWindow">
      <name>Inter</name>
      <size>13</size>
      <weight>medium</weight>
    </font>
    <!-- AQ-GENERATED-END fonts -->

  </theme>

  <keyboard>

    <!-- labwc's own defaults: Alt+Tab, Alt+F4, Super+a maximise, Super+arrows
         snap, plus the volume and brightness keys. Keeping them means this file
         only has to describe what is different about Aquarius. -->
    <default />

    <!--
      The Aquarius search palette.

      This does not launch a program. It sends a message to the shell that is
      ALREADY running, telling it to show or hide its search box.
      `qs ipc call <target> <function>` is Quickshell's own mechanism for that.
      The target "search" and its function "toggle" are declared by the
      IpcHandler in the shell's components/search/FlowSearch.qml.

      Which shell instance gets the message is not an argument: `qs ipc` picks
      the instance running the configuration named by QS_CONFIG_PATH, which
      labwc inherited from the launcher. (Corrected on the bench 2026-09-01 —
      an earlier version passed "aquarius-shell" as a first positional, which
      `qs` reads as the TARGET name, and no such target exists. It would have
      silently done nothing.)

      If the palette does not open, ask the running shell what it offers:
          qs ipc show
    -->
    <keybind key="W-space">
      <action name="Execute" command="qs ipc call search toggle" />
    </keybind>

    <!--
      A terminal, always.

      This is the escape hatch, and it is why it is bound explicitly rather than
      left to labwc's defaults: if the bar fails, the dialog that appears tells
      the person to press Super+Return and type `qs`. That instruction has to be
      true even on a desktop where nothing else is working.

      ptyxis is Fedora's terminal and is part of the AquariusOS image.
    -->
    <keybind key="W-Return">
      <action name="Execute">
        <command>ptyxis</command>
      </action>
    </keybind>

    <!-- ====================================================================== -->
    <!-- BEGIN: the Aquarius app switcher. Added 2026-09-06.                     -->
    <!-- ====================================================================== -->
    <!--
      THE ONLY BLOCK IN THIS FILE THE APP SWITCHER OWNS. Everything it needs is
      between this banner and the matching END banner further down, including
      the one setting outside <keyboard>. Nothing else in the file was touched.

      This block is the aquarius-shell repository's, copied across on
      2026-09-06 — see the note about drift under the terminal binding above.
      Change one, change both; build_files/check-labwc-drift.sh fails the build
      if the two copies stop agreeing.

      ======================================================================
      WHAT THESE FIVE BINDINGS DO
      ======================================================================
      None of them launches a program. Each one sends a short message to the
      shell that is ALREADY running, telling it to show its app switcher and
      move the selection. `qs ipc call <target> <function>` is Quickshell's own
      mechanism for that; the target "switcher" and its functions are declared
      by the IpcHandler in the shell's components/switcher/AppSwitcher.qml.

      If a key seems to do nothing, ask the running shell what it offers:
          qs ipc show

      ======================================================================
      WHY BOTH SUPER AND ALT ARE BOUND, ALL THE TIME
      ======================================================================
      AquariusOS asks "Mac or Windows?" once, at first login, and that answer
      decides which physical key is which:

        Mac style      Aquarius Keys swaps the two keys beside the space bar, so
                       the key a Mac user reaches for with their thumb sends
                       SUPER. Command+Tab arrives here as W-Tab. (The
                       Windows-logo key becomes Option and sends Alt.)
        Windows style  nothing is swapped and the remapper does not even run.
                       Alt+Tab arrives here as A-Tab.

      Binding both means this file never has to change when somebody runs
      `aq keys mac` or `aq keys windows`. What DOES change is what the panel
      lists — applications in the Mac style, windows in the Windows style — and
      the shell reads that from ~/.config/aquarius/keys.conf itself, live, with
      no restart. One question, asked once; no second setting anywhere.

      ======================================================================
      WHY labwc's OWN SWITCHER IS NOT USED ANY MORE
      ======================================================================
      These bindings used to be NextWindow and PreviousWindow, which are
      labwc's own actions and draw labwc's own on-screen list. That list works,
      but it is a plain list of window titles in labwc's theme, and it cannot
      group five windows of one editor into one application the way a Mac does.
      The Aquarius switcher is drawn by the shell, from the same icon theme and
      the same colours as the dock, and it knows about applications.

      labwc's list is switched off below, outside </keyboard>, so that nothing
      can put two switchers on screen at once.

      ======================================================================
      HOW IT KNOWS YOU LET GO OF THE KEY
      ======================================================================
      It is not in this file, and that surprises people, so: the shell sees the
      release ITSELF. Its panel asks the compositor for the keyboard while it is
      on screen, and because the Super key on its own is bound to nothing here,
      labwc passes the release of it straight through to whoever has the
      keyboard — which by then is the panel. The whole explanation, and the two
      approaches that were tried first, are in the aquarius-shell repository at
      docs/app-switcher.md.

      ⚠️ DO NOT ADD A BINDING FOR Super_L OR Alt_L HERE. Binding the modifier on
      its own would make labwc swallow its press, and a swallowed press means a
      swallowed release — which is the one event this whole feature depends on.
    -->

    <!-- Mac keyboard style: Command+Tab and Command+Shift+Tab. -->
    <keybind key="W-Tab">
      <action name="Execute" command="qs ipc call switcher next" />
    </keybind>

    <keybind key="W-S-Tab">
      <action name="Execute" command="qs ipc call switcher prev" />
    </keybind>

    <!--
      Command+` — step through the windows of the app you are already in,
      without putting anything on screen. The key to the left of the 1 on a US
      keyboard; labwc's name for it is "grave".
    -->
    <keybind key="W-grave">
      <action name="Execute" command="qs ipc call switcher cycle" />
    </keybind>

    <!--
      Windows keyboard style: Alt+Tab and Alt+Shift+Tab.

      These REPLACE labwc's built-in Alt+Tab, which arrives from the
      <default /> at the top of this section. When two keybinds name the same
      key, labwc keeps the last one written down and drops the earlier — so
      these lines, coming after it, are the ones that win. (Checked in labwc
      0.20.2's own source: deduplicate_key_bindings() in src/config/rcxml.c.)
    -->
    <keybind key="A-Tab">
      <action name="Execute" command="qs ipc call switcher next" />
    </keybind>

    <keybind key="A-S-Tab">
      <action name="Execute" command="qs ipc call switcher prev" />
    </keybind>

    <!-- Leave the desktop and go back to the login screen, where GNOME is one
         click away. This is the "get me out of here" key. -->
    <keybind key="W-S-e">
      <action name="Exit" />
    </keybind>

    <!-- ==================================================================== -->
    <!-- BEGIN: the lock screen (Ctrl+Cmd+Q, and Win+L). Added 2026-09-06.  -->
    <!-- ==================================================================== -->
    <!--
      Lock the screen.

      This does not launch a program. It sends a message to the shell that is
      ALREADY running, which is the whole reason the lock is instant: there is
      no Qt to start and no QML to load, so the screens are covered on the next
      frame rather than most of a second later, by which time you have stood up
      and walked away.

      The target "lock" and its function "lock" are declared by the IpcHandler
      in lock/LockLayer.qml. Same mechanism, and the same trap, as the search
      palette above: `qs ipc` takes NO configuration name, only the target and
      the function, and it finds the right instance from QS_CONFIG_PATH.

      TWO BINDINGS, ONE PER KEYBOARD STYLE — decided by Royce 2026-09-07.

        C-W-q   Mac mode: Ctrl+Command+Q, exactly the key macOS itself uses.
                (Command is Super here: Aquarius Keys maps it so.)
        W-l     Windows mode: Win+L, exactly the key Windows uses.

      WHY IT IS NOT Command+L. The first version bound only W-l and said "one
      binding covers both styles". It did not: Aquarius Keys' Mac set turns
      Command+L into Ctrl+L everywhere, for the browser address-bar habit, so
      labwc never saw the key and the lock never fired from the keyboard
      (bench, 2026-09-07). Royce kept the address bar. Both bindings are live
      in both modes — Ctrl+Win+Q in Windows mode is harmless — but only the
      right one is reachable in each, and only the right one is documented.

      ⚠️ Aquarius Keys' mac.yaml must never map C-Super-q, or this dies again
      silently. The image's build checks that (75-aquarius-keys.sh).

      If nothing happens, ask the running shell what it offers:
          qs ipc show
    -->
    <keybind key="C-W-q">
      <action name="Execute" command="qs ipc call lock lock" />
    </keybind>
    <keybind key="W-l">
      <action name="Execute" command="qs ipc call lock lock" />
    </keybind>
    <!-- ==================================================================== -->
    <!-- END: the lock screen                                                 -->
    <!-- ==================================================================== -->

  </keyboard>

  <!--
    labwc's own window switcher, switched off.

    The five bindings above no longer use NextWindow or PreviousWindow, so this
    list should never appear on its own. This turns it off anyway, belt and
    braces: if some future binding or a stray default ever reaches those actions
    again, the result would be TWO switchers on screen at the same time, and
    that is a confusing thing to debug from a screenshot.

    `show="no"` on the <osd> element is labwc 0.20's spelling for it — checked
    against labwc-config(5) at 0.20.2, the version this image builds. There is
    no `<windowSwitcher show="">` attribute; the attribute belongs to <osd>.
  -->
  <windowSwitcher>
    <osd show="no" />
  </windowSwitcher>
  <!-- ====================================================================== -->
  <!-- END: the Aquarius app switcher                                          -->
  <!-- ====================================================================== -->

  <mouse>

    <!--
      labwc's own mouse bindings: dragging a title bar to move a window,
      edge-resize, click to focus and raise, alt-drag to move. Keeping
      <default /> here is the whole point of this line and it is easy to lose:
      this section WITHOUT it replaces labwc's mouse behaviour entirely
      rather than adding to it, and the desktop would keep its right-click menu
      while losing the ability to drag a window by its title bar. So this
      section only describes what is different about Aquarius.
    -->
    <default />

    <!--
      Right-click the empty desktop and get the Aquarius menu.

      "Root" is labwc's name for the desktop itself, the part with no window on
      it. "root-menu" is the menu id declared in menu.xml next to this file. If
      that file is missing, or its id does not match, labwc silently falls back
      to its own built-in menu (Terminal / Reconfigure / Exit) — which is what
      the bench saw, and which is why menu.xml matters more than this binding
      does.

      Left-click on the root does nothing, and the <mousebind> below is what
      makes that true. This used to say left-click was "left unbound" — but
      <default /> above loads labwc's own bindings, and those bind Left (and
      Middle) on the desktop to open the menu too
      (include/config/default-bindings.h). On the bench, 2026-09-07, that showed
      up as a left click behaving like a right click. `None` is labwc's
      documented way to cancel one inherited binding (rcxml.c: "cancel a default
      binding by using the None action"), and it leaves the window and titlebar
      defaults from <default /> untouched. AquariusOS has no desktop icons, by
      Royce's decision, so a left click on the wallpaper should do nothing.
    -->
    <context name="Root">
      <mousebind button="Right" action="Press">
        <action name="ShowMenu" menu="root-menu" />
      </mousebind>
      <mousebind button="Left" action="Press">
        <action name="None" />
      </mousebind>
    </context>

  </mouse>

  <!-- Resolve's main editor asks for no frame and can restore a maximized
       state of its own. On the bench this left no usable title bar for moving
       or restoring the window. Give only the project editor our normal frame
       and restore it once when it appears. The class, normal-window type and
       project-title pattern together exclude the splash and dialogs.

       No fixed size and no ignoreConfigureRequest: Resolve may still resize
       itself, and the user may maximize it afterwards. FitToOutput also keeps
       older saved geometry from putting the controls beyond the screen. -->
  <windowRules>
    <windowRule identifier="resolve">
      <action name="FitToOutput" />
    </windowRule>
    <!-- Aquarius labwc extension: Resolve's charcoal frame, preserving the
         desktop's button placement and dimensions. Match class/type so colors
         also apply when the project title arrives after decoration creation. -->
    <windowRule identifier="resolve" type="normal"
                decorationColors="#222226 #202024 #101012 #101012 #dedee2 #a0a0a8" />
    <windowRule identifier="resolve" title="DaVinci Resolve* - *" type="normal" serverDecoration="yes">
      <action name="UnMaximize" direction="both" />
      <action name="FitToOutput" />
    </windowRule>
  </windowRules>

</labwc_config>
